p['urgent_extra']='Call your oncology team immediately for new breathlessness, persistent diarrhoea, blood or black stools, yellow eyes, dark urine, severe tiredness or dizziness, thirst and frequent urination, unusual weakness, reduced urine or a spreading rash. Tell every healthcare professional that you receive immunotherapy, even after it stops. Vomiting blood, heavy bleeding, sudden severe abdominal or chest pain, stroke symptoms, seizure, collapse or major breathing difficulty need 112 / 999.'
p['teachback']='Can you explain which medicine is immunotherapy, why varices, blood pressure and urine are checked, which symptoms need urgent contact, and why the alert card remains important after treatment stops?'
p['consent_support']='Discuss expected benefit and uncertainty, alternatives including not proceeding, liver disease and variceal bleeding, immune toxicity that may be delayed or need lifelong hormone replacement, vascular/kidney/bowel/healing risks, fertility and pregnancy prevention, IV versus thigh administration and your right to reconsider.'
p['questions'] += [dict(q='Why do I need a check for varices?',a='Liver disease can cause enlarged veins in the food pipe or stomach that may bleed. Bevacizumab can increase bleeding risk. The team checks for varices and treats any relevant risk before starting; having the test alone does not mean the risk has been controlled.'),dict(q='Does an abnormal liver test always mean immune hepatitis?',a='No. Liver cancer, underlying liver disease, infection, blocked bile flow and medicines can all affect results. The team compares results with your baseline and investigates the cause before deciding how each medicine changes.'),dict(q='Can one medicine be held while the other continues?',a='Sometimes. The reason for holding treatment, your general condition and the source rules for each medicine determine the plan. Never assume that stopping one means the other is automatically safe to continue.'),dict(q='Do immune side effects stop when treatment stops?',a='No. They can begin or persist afterwards. Keep the immunotherapy alert card and report new symptoms, including to doctors outside oncology.'),dict(q='Does the thigh injection replace every drip?',a='No. Atezolizumab may be given into the thigh, but bevacizumab remains an infusion into a vein. The two atezolizumab formulations have different doses and must be prescribed correctly.')]
p['support']=[item('Varices and bleeding','Screening and any required variceal treatment must be reviewed before starting. Call urgently for black stools or blood; vomiting blood or heavy bleeding needs emergency help.',['nccp','atezo-smpc']),item('Blood pressure and urine','Blood pressure and urine-protein checks are part of each cycle. Continue prescribed blood-pressure medicines and ask before changing them. A positive urine dipstick may need a timed urine collection.',['nccp','bev-smpc']),item('Immune alert card','Carry the supplied immunotherapy alert card during treatment and afterwards. New symptoms can need urgent treatment; do not wait for routine blood tests.',['atezo-smpc']),item('Support medicines','Both medicines have minimal sickness risk and routine preventive anti-sickness medicines or premedication are not usually required. The team individualises symptom treatment; do not self-treat new significant diarrhoea without advice.',['nccp','antiemetic']),item('Surgery, dental work and healing','Tell oncology before planned procedures and tell your dentist about bevacizumab. Wounds must heal and the team must agree an interruption/restart plan.',['bev-smpc']),item('Pregnancy prevention and breastfeeding','Discuss fertility and pregnancy prevention. Atezolizumab requires effective contraception for five months after the last dose and bevacizumab for six months; use the longer interval when both apply. Do not breastfeed during treatment or for at least six months after bevacizumab.',['atezo-smpc','bev-smpc'])]
p['chapters']=[chapter('treatment-purpose','Understanding the combination','Confirm the treatment aim and source eligibility.',[item('Treatment aim',p['purpose']),item('Liver health matters','The team checks liver function, cirrhosis, viral-hepatitis management and any ascites or confusion. The pivotal study mainly supports patients with Child-Pugh A liver function; poorer liver function needs specialist discussion.',['nccp','atezo-smpc']),item('Two medicine decisions',p['phases_note'])]),chapter('before-treatment','Preparing for the first treatment','Bleeding-risk review is a separate safety step.',[item('Varices',p['support'][0]['text'],['nccp','atezo-smpc']),item('Baseline checks','Blood count, kidneys, liver, glucose, thyroid function, urine protein, blood pressure and cardiac history are reviewed. Other tests depend on symptoms, earlier treatment and medicines.'),item('Consent and route',p['consent_support'])]),chapter('treatment-visit','Your treatment visit','The medicines follow a defined sequence.',[item('IV or thigh atezolizumab',p['administration_text']),item('During administration','Tell the nurse immediately about rash, swelling, breathlessness, chest tightness, dizziness or pain at the injection or infusion site. Do not wait until the visit ends.',['atezo-smpc','bev-smpc']),item('Before every cycle','Blood count, kidney/liver profile, glucose and urine protein are checked before every cycle. Blood pressure is checked before each cycle and after treatment. Thyroid tests are scheduled every six weeks.')]),chapter('between-visits','Care between visits','Symptoms can need review between blood tests.',p['support'][1:5]),chapter('after-treatment','Follow-up after treatment','Continue the contact and alert-card plan.',[item('Delayed immune effects',p['questions'][-2]['a'],['atezo-smpc']),item('Continuing support','The team follows ongoing liver, kidney, blood-pressure and hormonal problems and agrees medicine stop dates and next cancer reviews.'),item('Pregnancy prevention',p['support'][-1]['text'],['atezo-smpc','bev-smpc'])])]
